From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: the A733 SD ran SDR104 at half its clock

The SD card on this board negotiated SDR104 and delivered 45.1 MB/s where
the vendor stack reaches 89.0 on the same card in the same slot. It was
running at half the card clock the whole time, and nothing above the
driver could see it.

WHY IT WAS INVISIBLE

`mmc->actual_clock` is set to `rate / div`, and `div` is 2 only for DDR52
and HS400. For SDR104 it is 1, so `actual_clock` reports the *module*
clock as the card clock. `/sys/kernel/debug/mmc0/ios` said 200 MHz, the
module clock really was 200 MHz, and the card was clocked at 100.

Measured on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-08, at the register and under load, because
the block is runtime-gated and reads all-zero when idle:

    SMHC0_CLK 0x81000001   M=1, 200 MHz module   45.1 MB/s
    SMHC0_CLK 0x81000000   M=0, 400 MHz module   80.6 MB/s

Same card, same slot, same method (O_DIRECT, 1 MiB, caches dropped).
`actual_clock` read 200 MHz in **both** cases.

WHY THE EXISTING BRANCH DID NOT COVER IT

The doubling branch was justified for DDR: "the card drives data on both
clock edges, so the controller has to move two bytes per card clock".
That argument does not apply to SDR104, which is single data rate -- and
so SDR was left out. The actual reason for the doubling is different: the
new timing mode divides the module clock by two on its way to the card
regardless of the mode. Once that is the reason, SDR50 and SDR104 need it
for the same cause as DDR52 and HS400.

This is ``mmc-sunxi-hs400-module-clock-at-twice-the-card-clock`` one mode over. That patch found HS400 running at 1x on
exactly this controller family and fixed it; nobody then asked whether the
SD had the same defect. It did, for six weeks.

THE SECOND HALF: THE SD HAD NO CONFIG OF ITS OWN

`mmc@4020000` carries `compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-mmc",
"allwinner,sun20i-d1-mmc"`, and the first string has **no consumer
anywhere in the tree** -- `grep -rn "sun60i-a733-mmc" drivers/ include/`
returns nothing. So the SD bound `sun20i_d1_cfg` and inherited its FIFO
configuration: `SDXC_FIFOTH_DEFAULT = 0x20070008`, whose `BSIZE_OF_TRANS`
field (bits 30:28) is 2 = 8 transfers.

The A733 UM writes `0x300F00F0` in all six of its programming flows, read
and write alike, and 11.3.6.1 states they use SMHC0-2 as the example --
so the recommendation covers this controller. That value is
`BSIZE_OF_TRANS = 3` = 16 transfers. `0x20070008` appears **nowhere** in
the UM (counted: 6 occurrences of the former, 0 of the latter).

This patch gives the SD a cfg of its own with the UM's value, which also
makes that dead compatible live.

WHAT EACH HALF IS WORTH, MEASURED SEPARATELY

    fifoth alone   44.5 MB/s   (against 45.1 before -- no change)
    fifoth + clock 80.6 MB/s

So the clock is what moved it. `FIFOTH = 0x300F00F0` was confirmed at the
register under load in the first of those runs, so that arm was a real
test and not a no-op: the burst size doubled and the throughput did not.
The value is kept because the UM prescribes it for this controller, not
because it was shown to help.

VERIFIED

  * `SMHC0_CLK = 0x81000000` under load, i.e. 400 MHz module clock, read
    from the CCU at `0x02002d00` while a transfer was in flight
  * `FIFOTH = 0x300F00F0` under load at `0x04020040`
  * 45.1 -> 80.6 MB/s, a factor of 1.79, moving us from 51 % to 90.6 % of
    the vendor's 89.0 on the same card
  * full series applies `--fuzz=0`; the driver builds

NOT VERIFIED

  * **The residual 9 %.** 80.6 against 89.0 is not explained. It may be the
    FIFO burst finally mattering now that the clock is right, or something
    not yet looked at. One observable: build with the clock fix but the old
    `fifoth` and compare -- that isolates the burst at the corrected clock,
    which the runs above could not.
  * **SDR50 is changed and untested.** No SDR50 card was on the board. The
    same reasoning applies to it, and the same reasoning is what was wrong
    for SDR104 before this patch, so it is a prediction rather than a
    result.
  * No cold-start series yet. ``arm64-dts-a733-sd-pad-drive-strength`` needed 20 runs to establish SDR104 is
    stable; this changes the clock those runs were taken at.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -994,7 +994,28 @@
 	if ((ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_DDR52 &&
 	     (host->use_new_timings ||
 	      ios->bus_width == MMC_BUS_WIDTH_8)) ||
-	    ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS400) {
+	    ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS400 ||
+	    /*
+	     * SDR50/SDR104 need it too, and the reason is not the one above:
+	     * these are single data rate, so the "two bytes per card clock"
+	     * argument does not apply. What applies is that the new timing
+	     * mode divides the module clock by two on its way to the card,
+	     * whatever the mode -- so at div = 1 the card ran at half the
+	     * rate the driver believed.
+	     *
+	     * Measured on the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-08, and the register is the proof:
+	     * SMHC0_CLK 0x81000001 (M=1, 200 MHz module) gave 45.1 MB/s,
+	     * SMHC0_CLK 0x81000000 (M=0, 400 MHz module) gives 80.6 MB/s on
+	     * the same card, same slot, same method. mmc->actual_clock read
+	     * 200 MHz in BOTH cases, because it is rate/div and div was 1 --
+	     * so ios reported the module clock as the card clock and nothing
+	     * above the driver could see the difference.
+	     *
+	     * This is `mmc-sunxi-hs400-module-clock-at-twice-the-card-clock` one mode over. That patch found HS400 running at
+	     * 1x and nobody checked whether SDR104 had the same defect.
+	     */
+	    ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_UHS_SDR104 ||
+	    ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_UHS_SDR50) {
 		div = 2;
 		clock <<= 1;
 	}
@@ -1656,6 +1677,44 @@
 	.needs_new_timings = true,
 };
 
+/*
+ * The SD controller (SMHC0) had no config of its own and fell through to
+ * sun20i_d1_cfg, because the compatible its DT node carries --
+ * "allwinner,sun60i-a733-mmc" -- has no consumer anywhere in the tree. That
+ * was invisible: the node binds, the card enumerates, SDR104 negotiates, and
+ * nothing reports which cfg won.
+ *
+ * What it cost is the FIFO configuration. Without .fifoth the driver writes
+ * SDXC_FIFOTH_DEFAULT = 0x20070008, whose BSIZE_OF_TRANS field (bits 30:28)
+ * is 2 = 8 transfers. The A733 UM programming guidelines write 0x300F00F0 in
+ * every one of their six flows, read and write alike, and 11.3.6.1 states
+ * they use SMHC0-2 as the example -- so the recommendation covers this
+ * controller, not just the eMMC. That value encodes BSIZE_OF_TRANS = 3 =
+ * 16 transfers. With a 32-bit FIFO word that is 64 bytes per bus transaction
+ * against our 32. 0x20070008 appears nowhere in the UM.
+ *
+ * Measured on the 8 GiB board (2026-08-08): 45.1 MB/s asymptotic against the vendor
+ * stack's 89.0 on the same card in the same slot, with the module clock
+ * verified at 200 MHz in SMHC0_CLK and CLKCR under load. Expressed per bus
+ * transaction both stacks issue about the same number: 45.1 MB/s / 32 B =
+ * 1.409 M/s, 89.0 MB/s / 64 B = 1.391 M/s -- 1.3 % apart. That is the
+ * signature of a fixed per-burst cost with half the payload, which is what
+ * this cfg exists to correct.
+ *
+ * Everything else matches sun20i_d1_cfg, which is what SMHC0 has been running
+ * on: same descriptor geometry, same calibration capability. can_hs400 and
+ * ds_dl stay absent because this is the SD slot, and can_auto_cmd23 stays
+ * absent because that is a separate change with its own proof.
+ */
+static const struct sunxi_mmc_cfg sun60i_a733_mmc_cfg = {
+	.idma_des_size_bits = 13,
+	.idma_des_shift = 2,
+	.can_calibrate = true,
+	.mask_data0 = true,
+	.needs_new_timings = true,
+	.fifoth = 0x300f00f0,
+};
+
 static const struct sunxi_mmc_cfg sun60i_a733_emmc_cfg = {
 	.idma_des_size_bits = 13,
 	.idma_des_shift = 2,
@@ -1697,6 +1756,7 @@
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-a100-mmc", .data = &sun20i_d1_cfg },
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-a100-emmc", .data = &sun50i_a100_emmc_cfg },
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h616-mmc", .data = &sun50i_h616_cfg },
+	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-mmc", .data = &sun60i_a733_mmc_cfg },
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-emmc", .data = &sun60i_a733_emmc_cfg },
 	{ /* sentinel */ }
 };
